# Local settings. `just` loads a `.env` beside the justfile into every recipe
# (`set dotenv-load`), and git ignores it. Copy this file to `.env` and uncomment
# what you need; a variable already set in your shell wins over the file.
# `just where` prints what the recipes will use.

# --- The lakehouse's Parquet in an S3-compatible bucket (docs/WAREHOUSE.md) ---
#
# Unset, the Parquet sits under $LAKEHOUSE_DIR/data/, beside the catalog. Set, it
# goes to this bucket and prefix; the catalog stays a local file either way.
#
# - Choose before the first `just ingest`: the catalog records its data path and
#   refuses any other, so switching an existing landing zone is not a toggle.
# - `just export-data` and `just restore-history` refuse while it is set. The
#   release is built from a landing zone on disk.
#
# The values below match the SeaweedFS container docs/WAREHOUSE.md starts.
#LAKEHOUSE_DATA_PATH=s3://lake/modern-data-stack/
# The store's URL, scheme included: http:// turns TLS off, https:// keeps it.
#LAKEHOUSE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8333
#AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
#AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=testtest
# Optional. Most S3-compatible stores ignore the region, but a request signature
# still names one.
#AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# --- The DuckLake catalog in Postgres (docs/WAREHOUSE.md) ---
#
# Unset, the catalog is $LAKEHOUSE_DIR/catalog.duckdb. Set, DuckLake keeps its
# tables in this database instead, under LAKEHOUSE_METADATA_SCHEMA. Orthogonal to
# the block above: the catalog and the Parquet move independently.
#
# - Choose before the first `just ingest`, for the same reason as the data path.
# - `just export-data` and `just restore-history` refuse while it is set.
# - The URL carries no password, and one with a password in it is refused: libpq
#   reads PGPASSWORD, and that is what reaches DuckDB, dbt and dlt alike.
#
# `docker compose` reads this same file, so PGPASSWORD is also what the Postgres
# container is created with. `just compose-up` starts it.
#PGPASSWORD=change-me
#LAKEHOUSE_CATALOG=postgres://mds@127.0.0.1:5432/lakehouse
# Optional. The schema holding the ducklake_* tables; the default is `lakehouse`.
#LAKEHOUSE_METADATA_SCHEMA=lakehouse

# --- Dagster's own storage in Postgres (docs/RUNNING_AS_A_SERVICE.md) ---
#
# Read by `deploy/dagster.yaml`, and only when DAGSTER_HOME names that
# directory: `DAGSTER_HOME=$PWD/deploy just serve`, which is what the container
# does. The default `.dagster/` instance ignores every line below and keeps its
# run, event and schedule storage in SQLite files beside its config.
#
# One Postgres role owns both databases, so this is the PGPASSWORD above. The
# `dagster` database is created by deploy/postgres/init.sql when the volume is
# first initialised — `just compose-down volumes` is the only way to run it again.
#
# Install the driver first: `just deploy-deps`.
#DAGSTER_POSTGRES_HOST=127.0.0.1
#DAGSTER_POSTGRES_USER=mds
#DAGSTER_POSTGRES_DB=dagster
# Compute logs and run artifacts stay on a filesystem — Dagster keeps neither in
# Postgres. Absolute, and outside the checkout: it is run output, not source.
#DAGSTER_STORAGE_DIR=/var/tmp/mds-dagster
